  {
    "id": "aim.orderedfields.04.negation-partial-order",
    "type": "counterexample",
    "stage": 4,
    "topic": "Negating comparisons in total orders",
    "difficulty": "introductory",
    "front": "Why do the comparison-negation flips require a total (linear) order rather than merely a partial order?",
    "back": "A partial order can contain incomparable elements. For sets, $A\\nsubseteq B$ does not imply $B\\subseteq A$.",
    "source": {
      "tex": "output/pdf/ordered_field_reference.tex",
      "lean": "Aim/OrderTheory/TotalOrder.lean",
      "declaration": "IsLETotal.le_total"
    },
    "prerequisites": [
      "aim.orderedfields.04.negation-trichotomy"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "counterexample",
      "negation",
      "partial-order",
      "total-order",
      "ordered-fields"
    ],
    "explanation": "For example, {1} is not a subset of {2}, and {2} is not a subset of {1}. Totality is precisely what excludes this third possibility."
  },
  {
    "id": "aim.orderedfields.04.push-neg",
    "type": "concept",
    "stage": 4,
    "topic": "Negating comparisons in total orders",
    "difficulty": "introductory",
    "front": "In Lean over a total (linear) order, what does the `push_neg` tactic do to negated comparisons?",
    "back": "It rewrites them into positive complements: for example, $\\lnot(a\\le b)$ becomes $b<a$, $\\lnot(a<b)$ becomes $b\\le a$, and $\\lnot(a\\ne b)$ becomes $a=b$. Use `push_neg at h` to rewrite a hypothesis.",
    "source": {
